WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.220
Our number system bundles groups of ten

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:08.054
Twenty three contains two complete bundles of ten and three single units

00:00:08.054 --> 00:00:14.500
The digit two counts tens, while the digit three counts ones; their positions determine their values.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.323
Trade one bundle of ten for ten loose units

00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:23.629
We now have one ten and thirteen ones, still twenty three

00:00:23.629 --> 00:00:29.500
Regrouping changes the representation while preserving the amount, which is why carrying and borrowing work.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.218
The same bundling idea extends leftward

00:00:33.218 --> 00:00:37.650
In four hundred twenty, four counts hundreds and two counts tens

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:44.500
These bars count bundles of different sizes, and the zero records that there are no loose ones.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.656
Multiplying forty two by ten makes every unit ten times as large

00:00:50.656 --> 00:00:55.106
Four tens become four hundreds, and two ones become two tens

00:00:55.106 --> 00:01:00.367
The digits move one place left because every adjacent place differs by ten.
